<!--
  ⚠️ 這份 profile 在本技術棧下無效，且**未接進任何啟動腳本**。保留作為調查證據。

  實測（2026-08-18）：未啟用 SROS2 時，plugin 會初始化並建立稽核檔；
  一旦啟用 SROS2，rmw_fastrtps_shared_cpp 會自行組出 participant 的
  dds.sec.* 屬性清單，其中不含 logging plugin，本檔的 propertiesPolicy
  不被保留。RMW_FASTRTPS_USE_QOS_FROM_XML=1 亦無效。
  完整證據見 文件/DDS_Security_audit_log_不可用_2026-08-18.md。

  以下屬性名稱仍具參考價值：它們是從安裝版二進位與 header 抽出的，
  而非文件推測。log_level 與 logging_file 這兩個拼法在二進位中不存在，
  寫錯時 XML 解析器會照收然後靜默忽略。
-->
<!--
  Fast DDS participant profile that turns on the built-in DDS Security audit
  log. This is the missing source behind sros_auth_fail_rate and
  sros_permission_deny_rate: across the 1,100-session campaign the deny adapter
  read 249,670 lines and classified none of them, because it was following the
  generic stack stdout and no dedicated security sink existed.

  Property names and accepted values are taken from the installed build, not
  from documentation:
    /opt/ros/jazzy/include/fastrtps/fastdds/rtps/security/logging/LogOptions.h
    /opt/ros/jazzy/include/fastrtps/fastdds/rtps/security/logging/LoggingLevel.h
  and confirmed present as literals in /opt/ros/jazzy/lib/libfastrtps.so.
  The suffixes are logging_level and log_file; log_level and logging_file do
  not appear in the binary and would be silently ignored.

  Records arrive as BuiltinLoggingType, which carries facility 0x0A for
  security and authentication messages plus hostname, hostip, appname, procid
  and msgid. That fixed header and source identity is what lets the classifier
  trust a record, instead of parsing arbitrary application output where any
  node can print whatever it likes.

  This profile deliberately sets nothing but the logging properties. An earlier
  profile in this project set useBuiltinTransports=false with an
  interfaceWhiteList and stopped DDS working entirely while appearing to
  succeed, so transports and discovery are left at their defaults here. Fast
  DDS also falls back to defaults silently when a profile fails to parse, which
  is why tests/test_security.py asserts this file parses and carries every
  property, and why the caller must confirm messages still flow.
-->
<dds xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
  <profiles>
    <participant profile_name="sros2_firewall_security_log" is_default_profile="true">
      <rtps>
        <propertiesPolicy>
          <properties>
            <property>
              <name>dds.sec.log.plugin</name>
              <value>builtin.DDS_LogTopic</value>
            </property>
            <!-- Messages at or below this level are logged. WARNING keeps
                 authentication and permission failures while leaving out the
                 per-message INFORMATIONAL chatter. -->
            <property>
              <name>dds.sec.log.builtin.DDS_LogTopic.logging_level</name>
              <value>WARNING_LEVEL</value>
            </property>
            <!-- Absolute path. The runtime directory has to sit on the Linux
                 filesystem: ~/ros2_ws is a symlink onto the Windows 9p mount,
                 which already broke the telemetry socket with Errno 95. -->
            <property>
              <name>dds.sec.log.builtin.DDS_LogTopic.log_file</name>
              <value>/home/jesse/.local/share/sros2-firewall/live_runtime/dds_security_audit.log</value>
            </property>
            <!-- Do not republish security events onto a DDS topic. Distributing
                 them would put audit records back on the same bus the attacker
                 is on, and the file is what the adapter follows. -->
            <property>
              <name>dds.sec.log.builtin.DDS_LogTopic.distribute</name>
              <value>false</value>
            </property>
          </properties>
        </propertiesPolicy>
      </rtps>
    </participant>
  </profiles>
</dds>
